        "summary":  "Most AI projects fail due to organizational issues rather than technical problems, including integration challenges, poor governance, and misalignment with business goals. The article examines why initiatives derail and offers guidance on when to fix troubled projects versus shutting them down entirely. It identifies early warning signs and rescue frameworks that CIOs can apply before projects reach critical failure.",
        "execSummary":  "When AI initiatives underperform, the root cause is almost always organizational ΓÇö misaligned incentives, absent sponsors, or poorly defined success metrics ΓÇö not the underlying technology. This makes AI project rescue a leadership challenge first and an engineering challenge second. For leaders managing AI in customer support or service operations, the practical implication is that establishing a clear business owner (not just a tech owner) for each AI initiative is the single highest-leverage early decision. The article\u0027s key caveat is that the window to rescue a failing initiative closes faster than most leaders realize: waiting for the next quarterly review is often too late to prevent scope collapse or budget cancellation.",
